# WITHOUT THIS FILE THE IMAGE IS BUILT FROM THE DEVELOPER'S DISK.
#
# The Dockerfile installs dependencies from package.json/package-lock.json and only
# then does `COPY . .`. With no ignore list that COPY includes the host's own
# node_modules, which lands ON TOP of the freshly resolved tree and silently replaces
# it. The build then runs against whatever the developer happened to have installed
# locally, not against the lockfile.
#
# That is not hypothetical: it is exactly how a corrected
# `overrides.@softarc/native-federation` (4.4.1, exporting isUnderAnyDir) produced a
# build that still failed with "does not provide an export named 'isUnderAnyDir'" --
# the stale local 4.3.1 had overwritten it. It also hid the failure in the other
# direction, because the .stack-src build tree is a fresh clone with no node_modules,
# so the identical Dockerfile built fine there and broke here.
#
# It is also the single biggest cost in the build: the context upload was over 20
# seconds, almost entirely node_modules and .angular cache.
node_modules
.angular
dist
out
coverage

# VCS and editor noise -- never needed to build, and .git alone is tens of MB.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.vscode
.idea
*.swp

# Local env/secrets must never enter an image layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Test/report output.
playwright-report
test-results
*.log

# Docker's own files: changing the Dockerfile should not invalidate the context.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
